# Persistent names for the two CANable adapters on the HVAC Pi.
#
# WHY: can0/can1 are assigned in USB enumeration order, so they can swap on reboot.
# A swap would silently mislabel which bus a capture came from -- the same
# instability that already bit us with adapter indices on macOS, but worse, because
# nothing about the data announces that it happened.
#
# CAVEAT: these names follow the ADAPTER, not the wire. If you move the CAN leads
# between adapters, the names become wrong and nothing will warn you. Label the
# adapters physically, and note that tools can always re-verify by content --
# whichever bus carries 0x39D is the vehicle bus.
#
# Verify a name took:  ip -br link show type can

# Renaming alone is not enough. A replugged adapter comes back DOWN, state
# STOPPED, with no bitrate -- and the boot unit is Type=oneshot
# RemainAfterExit=yes, so systemd never re-runs it. Without the SYSTEMD_WANTS
# below, pulling the USB and putting it back leaves that bus dead until someone
# restarts the service by hand.
SUBSYSTEM=="net", ACTION=="add", ATTRS{idVendor}=="1d50", ATTRS{idProduct}=="606f", ATTRS{serial}=="003000294730510220353346", NAME="can-veh", TAG+="systemd", ENV{SYSTEMD_WANTS}+="ibooster-can-up@can-veh.service"
SUBSYSTEM=="net", ACTION=="add", ATTRS{idVendor}=="1d50", ATTRS{idProduct}=="606f", ATTRS{serial}=="003500434E56511820313534", NAME="can-yaw", TAG+="systemd", ENV{SYSTEMD_WANTS}+="ibooster-can-up@can-yaw.service"
